    "title": "Dan Levy returns to TV with Netflix crime comedy 'Big Mistakes'",
    "text": "Six years after 'Schitt's Creek,' the writer-actor tackles organized crime, dysfunctional family and the chaos in between. #k5evening NEW YORK CITY, N.Y. — Six years after wrapping the beloved Canadian comedy \"Schitt's Creek,\" Dan Levy is back on screen — and this time, the stakes are considerably higher than small-town social climbing. Levy stars in \"Big Mistakes,\" a new Netflix series now streaming, in which he plays a New Jersey man who, alongside his sister played by Taylor Ortega, becomes entangled in organized crime. The show marks a notable tonal shift for Levy, trading the warmhearted quirkiness of \"Schitt's Creek\" for something darker, more chaotic — and, by most accounts, just as funny. \"I write what I know,\" Levy said during a recent press event, \"And this is certainly a celebration of yelly families.\" That familial chaos is at the center of the show. Laurie Metcalf co-stars as Linda, the Ortega family matriarch, a role Metcalf says gave her room to cut loose. She described the freedom to have Linda \"fly off the handle whenever, wherever and at whoever might be next to her at the moment.\" Levy has said the series grew out of a genuine personal fear — the unsettling idea of being trapped, involuntarily, in criminal activity. \"Who would want to be trapped in criminal activity?\" he said. \"I think I'm just saying the quiet part out loud.\" The show also marks a breakout moment for the actress playing Taylor Ortega. Asked how she connects with the character, she answered simply: \"Closely.\" In a more expansive moment, she described understanding the feeling of believing \"everything is pointless because things didn't work out for you,\" and also knowing what it feels like to find your footing again. Despite the guns, threats and criminal entanglements, Levy says \"Big Mistakes\" is, at its core, a show about why families stick together even when they make each other miserable. \"No matter what kind of danger any of you are in,\" Levy said. \"It's not going to stop you from bickering with each other. That's baked into this family.\" Levy acknowledged that viewer reactions to the show's loud, combative family dynamic will likely depend on their own upbringing. \"If you come from a yelly family, this will feel immediately at home,\" he said. \"If you don't, this will probably feel quite alien.\" \"Big Mistakes\" is streaming now on Netflix.",
